NAME
       pwguess - try passwords against a login service (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       pwguess (-l USER | -L USERFILE) (-p PASSWORD | -P WORDFILE) ssh://HOST
       pwguess (-l USER | -L USERFILE) (-p PASSWORD | -P WORDFILE) ftp://HOST
       pwguess (-l USER | -L USERFILE) (-p PASSWORD | -P WORDFILE) http-form://HOST/PATH

DESCRIPTION
       pwguess signs in again and again with each user name and each password
       until one works (an online guessing attack). It is slow and very loud:
       every failed attempt ends up in the server's log, and in the range it
       makes the loudest noise there is. Real servers often lock accounts or
       block the address after a few failures.

       With http-form://HOST/PATH it posts user=NAME&pass=PASSWORD to a web
       sign-in page. A page that answers with a status from 200 to 399 took the
       password; 401 or 403 means it was wrong. A page that answers 429 (too
       many requests) is limiting guesses, and pwguess stops. Guessing only
       works against a page that never slows down or locks the account - the
       "no rate limit" mistake.

       Use it only on the range, and try quieter ways first (notes, backups,
       reused passwords).

OPTIONS
       -l USER       one user name
       -L USERFILE   a file with a user name on each line
       -p PASSWORD   one password
       -P WORDFILE   a file with a password on each line, such as
                     /usr/share/wordlists/range-common.txt

EXAMPLES
       pwguess -l dana -P /usr/share/wordlists/range-common.txt ssh://10.10.1.20
       pwguess -l manager -P /usr/share/wordlists/range-common.txt http-form://copperleaf.example/login

NOTES
       Guessing passwords on systems you don't own, or without written
       permission, is against the law in most countries.

SEE ALSO
       hashcrack(1), ssh(1), journalctl(1), curl(1)
